nss_ipv4/nss_ipv6: bound the conn_sync_many count by the message length

The sync-many response carries an array of per-connection statistics and
a count of how many it holds. Both the count and the size it is checked
against come out of the same firmware-written buffer:

	if (nicsm->count * sizeof(struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync) >= nicsm->size) {

so the test compares one firmware value with another and a response
declaring size = 0xffff, count = 0xffff walks straight through it. The
test is wrong even for a well-behaved firmware: ECM seeds size to
PAGE_SIZE, which is larger than the response buffer, so counts that pass
still overread.

Worse, the test guards only the driver's own loop. The handler also sets
ncm->cb to the registered sync-many callback, and ECM's

	for (i = 0; i < nicsm->count; i++)

in ecm_nss_ipv4_connection_sync_many_callback() has no check at all,
feeding every record into a connection-hash lookup and a conntrack
timeout update from NAPI context. With count = 0xffff and 116-byte
records that is ~7.4 MB read past the response buffer.

Bound the count where both consumers route through it - the message
handler - against ncm->len, which is the one quantity the host validated
against the receive buffer in nss_core_handle_nss_status_pkt(), and drop
the test it replaces. Clamping rather than returning is what keeps ECM's
unguarded loop safe without editing ECM.

Both families change together; the IPv4 and IPv6 paths are line-for-line
identical here.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/nss_ipv4.c
+++ b/nss_ipv4.c
@@ -120,7 +120,24 @@ static void nss_ipv4_rx_msg_handler(stru
 		nss_ipv4_stats_conn_sync(nss_ctx, &nim->msg.conn_stats);
 		break;
 
-	case NSS_IPV4_TX_CONN_STATS_SYNC_MANY_MSG:
+	case NSS_IPV4_TX_CONN_STATS_SYNC_MANY_MSG: {
+		/*
+		 * count and size are both written by the firmware, so the
+		 * count-against-size test inside the stats helper compares one
+		 * firmware value with another. Bound count by ncm->len, the
+		 * one quantity the host checked against the receive buffer -
+		 * and do it here, because the registered callback below
+		 * iterates the same count with no test of its own.
+		 */
+		uint32_t max = (ncm->len < sizeof(struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync_many_msg)) ? 0 :
+			(ncm->len - sizeof(struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync_many_msg)) / sizeof(struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync);
+
+		if (nim->msg.conn_stats_many.count > max) {
+			nss_warning("%px: conn_sync_many count %u exceeds message capacity %u",
+					nss_ctx, nim->msg.conn_stats_many.count, max);
+			nim->msg.conn_stats_many.count = max;
+		}
+
 		/*
 		 * Update driver statistics on connection sync many.
 		 */
@@ -128,6 +145,7 @@ static void nss_ipv4_rx_msg_handler(stru
 		ncm->cb = (nss_ptr_t)nss_ipv4_conn_sync_many_msg_cb;
 		break;
 	}
+	}
 
 	/*
 	 * Update the callback and app_data for NOTIFY messages, IPv4 sends all notify messages
